  "question": {
    "text": "The order of equivalent conductances at infinite dilution for LiCl, $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ and $\\mathrm{KCl}$ is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{LCl}>\\mathrm{NaCl}>\\mathrm{KCl}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{KCl}>\\mathrm{NaCl}>\\mathrm{LiCl}$",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{NaCl}>\\mathrm{KCl}>\\mathrm{LiCl}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\mathrm{LCl}>\\mathrm{KCl}>\\mathrm{NaCl}$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "KCl is more ionic than NaCl while NaCl is more ionic than LiCl\r\nAlso, more the ionic nature, more is the conductivity thus, order of equivalent conductance at infinite dilution is $\\mathrm{KCl}>\\mathrm{NaCl}>\\mathrm{LiCl}$"
  }
}